Allergic Bronchopulmonary Aspergillosis (ABPA)
Respiratory System
Refractory asthma, Brown mucous plugs, Central bronchiectasis

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: RPS-004
Reason for Visit: Acute flare of refractory asthma symptoms with expectoration of alarming brown mucous plugs and pleuritic pain, requiring urgent assessment.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage revealed a patient with a history of difficult-to-control asthma presenting with hemoptysis and brown sputum. Urgent assessment was required to differentiate from acute pulmonary embolism or severe infection prior to the next in-hours period.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: My asthma is out of control again, and I am coughing up these thick, brown lumps of mucus that hurt my chest.
History of Present Illness: Symptoms started gradually over the past two weeks with a chronic, productive cough. In the last 24 hours, the patient has experienced recurrent asthma exacerbations refractory to standard therapy, expectoration of thick, brownish mucous plugs, and mild hemoptysis. They describe pleuritic chest pain on deep inspiration and a low-grade fever during this acute flare-up. Unexplained weight loss has occurred over the last month.
Functional Status: Independent, but experiencing significant exertional dyspnea.
Social/Safety Concerns: Frequent sick days from work leading to financial stress.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 128/82 mmHg | HR: 95 bpm | Temp: 37.8 C | RR: 22 breaths/min | SpO2: 95% on room air | Wt: 68 Kg
General: Chronically unwell appearance, mild respiratory distress, frequent coughing.
Physical Exam: Auscultation reveals generalized wheezing and rhonchi bilaterally. Clubbing of the digits is noted on extremity examination. The patient is expectorating brown mucous plugs. Central bronchiectasis is suspected underlying the chronic presentation.
Environmental Observations: Evidence of dampness and possible mold in the bathroom area.
Medication Reconciliation: High-dose inhaled corticosteroids and long-acting beta-agonists utilized with poor current effect.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Allergic Bronchopulmonary Aspergillosis (ABPA) - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:

Cystic Fibrosis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Late onset of symptoms and absence of typical gastrointestinal involvement or family history.

Pulmonary Tuberculosis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Absence of night sweats, negative travel history, and presence of widespread wheeze rather than apical crackles.

Pulmonary Embolism - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Presence of purulent brown sputum, fever, and generalized wheeze.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Moderate

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presents with an acute exacerbation of refractory asthma complicated by the expectoration of brown mucous plugs, low-grade fever, and mild hemoptysis. The clinical picture is highly suspicious for ABPA. The severity of the bronchospasm and the presence of hemoptysis mandated an urgent after-hours home visit to rule out life-threatening causes of hemoptysis, provide potent systemic corticosteroids to suppress the intense allergic response, and prevent irreversible airway damage (bronchiectasis). Deferring assessment until the next in-hours period risked progressive respiratory failure and ongoing structural lung damage.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Commenced high-dose oral prednisolone (0.5 mg/kg). Advised urgent routine follow-up for specific IgE testing and HRCT imaging.
Positive Behavioral Change: Clean and dry the bathroom surfaces daily using a mold-inhibiting solution; doing this strictly removes fungal spores from your environment, which directly prevents the allergic triggering of your asthma.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None.
DME/Safety (Optional): None.
Follow-up: GP review within 3 days for blood tests (total serum IgE, Aspergillus-specific IgE) and imaging referrals.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate Emergency Department presentation if there is large volume hemoptysis (more than a tablespoon of blood), severe difficulty breathing, or sudden sharp chest pain.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: This is a presumed diagnosis based on clinical signs; formal confirmation requires specialized blood tests and a high-resolution CT scan.
Clinical Course: The acute allergic inflammation will respond to oral steroids, but long-term management requires strict adherence to medication to prevent permanent lung scarring.
SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Seek emergency care if you cough up fresh red blood in large amounts, or if your breathing becomes so labored you cannot walk across the room.
PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: The oral steroids are essential to shut down the aggressive allergic reaction in your lungs caused by the fungus, preventing further damage to the airways.
Actionable Steps: Take your oral steroids exactly as prescribed without skipping doses; this steady medication level keeps the severe inflammation suppressed, allowing your airways to heal and clear the mucous plugs.

